window.docContents[149].resolve({"/Foundations/Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis--Tempered-Distributions--Affine-pushforward":{"contents":"Every continuous real-affine equivalence  induces a\ncontinuous complex-linear equivalencecharacterized byThe construction composes the linear pushforward with distributional\ntranslation; Schwartz precomposition is assembled from Mathlib's corresponding\nlinear and translation operators. Internally, if  is the linear part, the\nfactorization is . This sign is forced by the\ntranslation convention:  moves support by , so\nthe parameter  adds the affine offset .\n\nFor  and , the composite has linear part\n and offset .\n\nAffine pushforward preserves identities and composition, and pushforward by\n is inverse to pushforward by :\n\nEvaluate each distribution on a Schwartz function. The statements reduce to\nidentity, composition, and inverse laws for precomposition by continuous affine\nequivalences.\n\nThe derivative rule sees only the linear part of , because translation\ncommutes with directional derivatives.\n\nIf  is the continuous linear part of , directional derivatives\ntransform contravariantly:\n\nWrite affine pushforward as linear pushforward followed by translation.\nTranslation commutes with directional derivatives, while the linear\npushforward chain rule replaces  by . Combining the two identities\ngives the formula.\n\nSupports, by contrast, see both parts of .\n\nAffine pushforward carries distributional support to its geometric image:\n\nThe linear part maps support to its linear image, and the following translation\nmoves that image by . The affine decomposition\n identifies the resulting set with the image under .\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Distributions and Fourier Analysis\u0009Tempered Distributions","header":"3.4.2.4. Affine pushforward","id":"/Foundations/Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis--Tempered-Distributions--Affine-pushforward"},"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws--Conservation-Laws":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Symmetric Systems and Conservation Laws","header":"7.5.2. Conservation Laws","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws--Conservation-Laws"},"/Parabolic-Equations/Classical-Heat-Flow/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Classical-Heat-Flow--Maximum___-Comparison___-and-Uniqueness":{"contents":"Parabolic equations propagate information forward from the initial face and\ninward from the spatial boundary. Their natural boundary is therefore not the\nentire boundary of a spacetime cylinder: the spatial interior of the\nterminal-time face is excluded, while its lateral boundary points remain.\nThe parabolic boundary encodes this causal geometry.\n\nThe maximum principle rules out a new positive maximum in the positive-time\ninterior. A small perturbation by  makes the differential\ninequality strict, compactness supplies a maximizer, and the spatial\nsecond-derivative test conflicts with the time derivative at that point.\nApplying the same result to differences yields comparison and uniqueness.\n\nFor an open spatial domain  and , the closed spacetime\ncylinder is .\n\nBoundary data are prescribed on only part of the boundary of this cylinder.\nThe maximum-principle argument finds a maximizer over the whole closed set\nand must then be able to exclude it from the region where the differential\ninequality holds, and that region is , terminal face\nincluded.\n\nThe parabolic boundary consists of the initial face\n and the lateral boundary\n. The terminal face\n is excluded.\n\nThe operator to which the argument applies need not be the Laplacian. What\nthe second-derivative test uses at a spatial maximum is only that each\nprincipal term is a second derivative in a fixed direction. The directions\nmay vary with the point, but they are held fixed while differentiating the\nspatial slice; no derivatives of the coefficient fields enter.\n\nFor a scalar field  on a real normed space, finitely many direction\nfields , and a drift , defineThe potential variant is . The definitions impose\nno sign condition on ; \nassumes .Writing  for coordinate  of direction , the\nprincipal matrix has entries .\nEvery symmetric positive-semidefinite matrix has such a factorization. The theorems use\nonly this semidefinite sign.\n\nFor the heat equation itself the factorization is the standard basis:\n gives , and .\n\nFor a field written with separate spatial and time arguments, the classical\nheat operator is . The heat maximum, comparison, and\nuniqueness statements use this operator directly.\n\nThe first principle is stated for the factored operator without zeroth-order\nterm, and only the semidefinite sign of the principal part enters. Its\nhypotheses are the template for the other maximum principles:\ncontinuity on the closed cylinder, classical differentiability at each point\nof , the differential inequality there, and a bound on\nthe parabolic boundary.\n\nLet  be nonempty and open with compact closure, and let .\nAssume  is continuous on the closed cylinder. At every\n, assume its spatial slice is  at ,\nits time slice is differentiable at , and .\nIf  on the parabolic boundary, then\n throughout the closed cylinder.The time derivative is required at  even at spatial interior points,\nwhere terminal boundary data are not prescribed. It is the two-sided\nderivative on the real line, not a one-sided derivative within . The\npotential variants use the same hypotheses; the heat versions ask for less at\nthe terminal face.\n\nFor , the perturbation \nsatisfies  and retains the boundary upper bound.\nIf its maximum on the compact cylinder exceeded , a maximizer would\nlie in . There the spatial gradient vanishes and every\ndirectional second derivative is nonpositive. The time derivative is\nnonnegative, including at , where comparison with earlier times gives\nthe same sign. Thus , a contradiction. Let\n to recover the bound for .\n\nA zeroth-order term  with  does not disturb the argument\nprovided the bound  is nonnegative. At a point where  the\nterm  is nonnegative, so it only makes the inequality \nharder to satisfy. This is where the sign restriction on  enters.\n\nIf , , , and  on the\nparabolic boundary, then  throughout the closed cylinder. Taking\n to be the maximum of the positive part of the boundary data gives\nEvans's Theorem 9(i).\n\nThe same perturbation  produces the additional\nterm , which has the favorable sign because\n. At a positive maximum the zeroth-order term is also\nnonnegative.\n\nReplacing  by  turns subsolutions into supersolutions and upper\nbounds into lower bounds. The sign condition on  flips with it: the\nminimum principle requires , precisely so that  is an\nadmissible upper bound for .\n\nIf , , , and  on the\nparabolic boundary, then  throughout the closed cylinder. This is\nthe dual minimum statement in Evans's Theorem 9(ii), with the sign restriction\non  required by the nonnegative zeroth-order coefficient.\n\nApply the maximum principle with potential to  and the nonnegative upper\nbound , then negate the resulting inequality.\n\nFor the heat operator,  and the principal part is the full Laplacian,\nso the sign restriction on  disappears. The statement is repeated for\nthe heat operator so that no factorization of the\nidentity matrix has to be supplied when it is invoked. It is also sharper at\nthe top of the cylinder: derivatives and the differential inequality are\nneeded only on the open slab . Continuity carries the\nbound from every shorter cylinder up to the terminal face.\n\nLet  be nonempty and open with compact closure, , and \ncontinuous on the closed cylinder. Suppose that at every\n the spatial slice is , the time slice is\ndifferentiable, and . If  on the parabolic\nboundary, then  throughout the closed cylinder.\n\nAt a spatial maximum , while at a positive-time maximum on\n one has . The perturbation\n reduces the non-strict case to this strict\ncontradiction on each cylinder  with ,\nwhere the hypotheses hold up to the top face. Every point of\n lies in such a cylinder, and the closed cylinder is the\nclosure of this open slab, so continuity gives the bound everywhere.\n\nNegating a supersolution of the heat equation produces a subsolution with the\nnegated bound.\n\nFor the heat operator, if  on \nand  on the parabolic boundary, then  on the closed\ncylinder. Unlike the potential case, no sign condition on  is needed.\n\nSet . Then , while the boundary\nhypothesis  becomes . The weak maximum principle gives\n throughout the cylinder, which is equivalent to .\n\nApplied to a difference , the maximum principle compares two\nfunctions instead of bounding one.\n\nIf  on  and  on the\nparabolic boundary, with the classical regularity required by the maximum\nprinciple, then  on the entire closed cylinder.\n\nSet . Linearity of the heat operator gives\n, and the boundary assumption gives  on the\nparabolic boundary. The weak maximum principle therefore yields \non the closed cylinder, which is exactly .\n\nUniqueness is comparison applied twice.\n\nCompactness of  supplies the point where the perturbed\nsolution attains its maximum. On an unbounded domain, behavior at infinity\nneeds a separate argument.\n\nLet  be nonempty and open with compact closure, and . Under the\nregularity hypotheses of , two\nclassical heat solutions with equal residual on  and equal\nparabolic boundary data agree throughout the closed cylinder:\n\nThe two solutions have the same heat residual and the same parabolic boundary\ndata. Comparison first gives ; exchanging the two solutions gives\n. Pointwise antisymmetry of the real order then gives  on the\nwhole closed cylinder.\n\nOn all of  there is no compact cylinder, and uniqueness fails\nwithout some control at infinity: Tychonoff's classical example is a nonzero\nsolution with zero initial data that grows faster than any Gaussian. The\nremedy in  is a growth bound of the form\n. Any fixed Gaussian rate  is allowed, however\nlarge, and the time horizon  is arbitrary.\n\nLet  and . Let  be continuous on ,\nwith  spatial slices and differentiable time slices on\n, and suppose  there. If\n for , thenwith suprema in the extended reals. The same equality holds with \nreplaced by .\n\nOnly the upper bound needs work, and the extended-real formulation makes the\ncase of initial data unbounded above trivial. So fix  with\n. On a short time interval, of length fixed by ,\ncompare  on a large ball with  plus a small multiple of the growing\nGaussian . This barrier\nsolves the heat equation, and on the sphere  it outgrows\n once  is large. The weak maximum principle on the ball\ngives  plus the small multiple, and letting the multiple tend\nto zero gives . The short intervals are then concatenated up\nto . The equality with the positive-time supremum uses continuity at\n.\n\nThe growth bound is needed only for , and the equation only at\ninterior times.\n\nLet  and  satisfy the regularity hypotheses of\n and solve the same\nforced equation  on .\nIf  and  for ,\nand , then  on .\n\nThe difference  solves the homogeneous equation, vanishes at\n, and satisfies . The\nmaximum principle applied to  and to  gives  and\n.\n\nThe weak principle says the maximum is attained on the parabolic boundary. It\ndoes not rule out an interior maximum as well. The strong principle says that\nan interior maximum at a positive time  forces the solution to be\nconstant on the whole cylinder below . The statement is only about the\npast. Boundary values imposed after  can still change the solution\nthere.\n\nThe strong principle is proved for a general operator in nondivergence form.\nThe coefficients multiply second derivatives directly and are never\ndifferentiated.\n\nFor a matrix field , a drift , and a potential ,When  and the spatial slice is\n at the point, this agrees with the factored operator.\n\nLet  be open and connected. Let , ,  be continuous on\n, with  symmetric and\n for some . Let  be\ncontinuous on , with  spatial slices and\ndifferentiable time slices on . Suppose  there and\n attains its maximum over the closed cylinder at  with\n and . If , or if  and\n, then  on\n. The minimum versions hold for , with\n in the case .\n\nPut  and . The sign conditions ensure that\n is a supersolution. The argument proves that positivity of  moves\nforward in time. If , a small cubic bump centred at  lies\nbelow  at time . Moved along a segment and damped exponentially\nin time, the bump remains a subsolution: uniform ellipticity and bounds on\nthe continuous coefficients over a compact piece of the cylinder fix the\ndamping rate. Comparison on the moving ball then keeps  at the end of\nthe segment. Any two points of the connected set  are joined by a finite\nchain of overlapping balls inside . So if  for some ,\nthen , which is false. Hence  on ,\nand continuity extends this to .The proof uses only comparison with explicit barriers. It needs neither boundedness of\n nor derivatives of the coefficients. It also uses derivatives only\nstrictly before , so a maximum at  is covered.\n\nFor the heat equation,  is the identity matrix, , , and\n. The strong principle then holds for extrema of either sign.\n\nLet  be open and connected, and let  be continuous on\n, with  spatial slices and differentiable time\nslices on . If  and  attains its maximum over\nthe closed cylinder at an interior point  with ,\nthen  is constant on . The same holds for a\nminimum of a supersolution.\n\nThe heat operator equals the matrix operator with coefficients\n, identically in . The identity matrix is symmetric with\nellipticity constant one, and constant coefficients are continuous.\n\nThe heat case also follows from the mean value formula on heat balls\n(), a route specific to the\nLaplacian.\n\nA consequence is infinite speed of propagation on bounded domains. With zero\nboundary values, a nonnegative initial temperature that is positive\nsomewhere makes the temperature strictly positive everywhere inside at every\npositive time.\n\nLet  be open and connected with compact closure. Let  be a\nclassical supersolution,  on , with\n on the parabolic boundary and  for some\n. Then  for every  and\n.\n\nBy the weak minimum principle  on the closed cylinder. If\n for some  and , then  is an interior\nminimum, and the strong principle makes  identically zero on\n. This contradicts .\n\nOn the whole line the same fact is visible in the kernel formula\n(), since the Gaussian is positive. On a\nbounded interval, the figure computes the zero-boundary solution from its\nsine series.\n\n\n\nThe Harnack inequality is a quantitative form of the strong principle. It\ncompares a nonnegative solution at an earlier time with the same solution at\na later time, uniformly over a compact subregion. The constant depends on the\ngeometry and the coefficients. It is chosen before the solution.\n\nLet  be open, and let  be connected with compact closure\n. Let . Let , ,  be\ncontinuous on , with  symmetric and uniformly elliptic;\n may have either sign. Then there is  with the following\nproperty. Let  be continuous on . Suppose  has\ncontinuous spatial first and second derivatives and a continuous time\nderivative on , and  there. Then\n\nThe proof uses the contact-set method of Krylov and Safonov, in the form\npresented by Imbert and Silvestre. Suppose a supersolution is small at some\npoint at a later time. Slide a family of paraboloids up from below until\nthey first touch the graph at earlier times. At each contact point the\nspatial Hessian of the solution is bounded below by that of the paraboloid.\nThe supersolution inequality then bounds the Jacobian of the contact map,\nusing only ellipticity and bounds on the trace and the drift. The change of\nvariables formula turns this into a lower bound on the measure of the set\nwhere the solution was small earlier. An expanding barrier and an iteration\nof this growth step give a measure estimate on nested cylinders. The upper\nside comes from propagating almost-maximal values forward to later balls.\nTogether they give the comparison on small cylinders, and a finite covering\nof  gives the constant. A potential of either sign is\nabsorbed by adding one spatial variable  and multiplying by an\nexponential in . The potential then becomes a drift in . The\ncontact argument needs a jointly  gradient. The classical hypotheses\nstated here are reached by averaging forward in time and letting the width\ntend to zero.The coefficients are only assumed continuous.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Classical Heat Flow","header":"6.2.5. Maximum, Comparison, and Uniqueness","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/Classical-Heat-Flow/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Classical-Heat-Flow--Maximum___-Comparison___-and-Uniqueness"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--H___lder-Spaces--Exponents-and-Quantitative-H___lder-Control":{"contents":"HolderSpace.Exponent records the classical range\n as a nonnegative real together with proofs of both\ninequalities. Keeping the range in the type prevents every analytic theorem\nfrom repeating the same side conditions.The endpoint  is included and is available as the numeral 1; it\nis exactly the Lipschitz case. The exponent  is excluded, so every\nexponent of this type gives continuity and not only an oscillation bound.\n\nFor metric spaces , a map  satisfies\nHolderWith C γ f whenMemHolder γ f means that such a finite nonnegative constant  exists.\nFor a function satisfying this predicate, Mathlib's nnHolderNorm γ f is the\nleast admissible nonnegative Hölder constant and is itself admissible.\nPDEAtlas uses this least constant as its Hölder seminorm.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Extension, Trace, and Embeddings\u0009Hölder Spaces","header":"4.3.3.1. Exponents and Quantitative Hölder Control","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--H___lder-Spaces--Exponents-and-Quantitative-H___lder-Control"}});